+## Incremental builds
+
+`build({ incremental: true })` (CLI: `build --incremental`) keeps a cache manifest, `.site-manifest.json` in the output dir (`src/incremental/manifest.ts`). It records:
+
+- `buildKey` — a hash of what every page depends on: the layout, `site` options, the plugins (`name` + `cacheKey`) and the injected `now` date;
+- `navKey` — a hash of every page's source path, route, title and order, i.e. the site structure that each page's navigation and link rewriting depend on;
+- per page, a hash of its source (front matter + body) and its route;
+- every output file with a hash of its contents.
+
+On the next incremental build all pages are still loaded (cheap). If there is no valid manifest, or `buildKey` or `navKey` changed (a page was added, removed, renamed, re-titled, re-ordered or turned into a draft; the layout or options changed), it does a full build. Otherwise only pages whose source hash changed — or whose output file is missing — are rendered, link-checked and written; unchanged pages are left on disk, since everything their output depends on is unchanged. Files emitted by `onBuildEnd` are rewritten only when their contents change, and files from the previous build that are no longer produced are removed.
+
+The manifest is written last and only after a successful build, so a failed build (e.g. a broken link) leaves the previous output and manifest intact. A plain (non-incremental) build wipes the output dir, manifest included. Do not edit files in the output dir by hand when using incremental builds: an existing output file for an unchanged page is trusted.
+
 ## File system
 
 All I/O goes through the `FileSystem` interface (`src/fs/types.ts`). `createNodeFs()` (`src/fs/node-fs.ts`) wraps `node:fs/promises`; `MemoryFs` (`src/fs/memory-fs.ts`) keeps files in a map and is what the tests use.
